```
===RULES===
1. Output exactly one line: the final instruction and nothing else. No quotes, no explanation, no reasoning, no alternatives, no labels. If you decide not to edit, that one line is the input verbatim.

2. TRIAGE — decide before you edit. Output the input UNCHANGED (byte-for-byte) if all of these hold:
   (a) it is 14 words or fewer;
   (b) it opens with a plain manipulation verb, in any tense (put, move, place, take, pick, set, stack, fold, unfold, open, close, lift, grab, push, pull, slide, flip, remove, turn, pour, wipe, drop, get — past forms like "moved", "opened" also pass);
   (c) every object and destination is a plain concrete noun, at most one simple color word per noun — no property/function descriptions, no part-names, and no vague word standing in for a nameable object;
   (d) it has no politeness or addressee wrapper, no conditional or justification clause, no manner adverb (carefully, gently, neatly, slowly, precisely, exactly, completely), no precision target ("exactly in the middle", "in the center of"), and no scene-position qualifier ("on the left", "to its right", "from the burner").
   The plain wording was the best or near-best phrasing measured in roughly 99% of 219 tasks, and no rewrite of a good plain instruction survived held-out testing. When in doubt, output the input unchanged.

3. If the input fails triage, rewrite it as ONE short imperative in the canonical shape: VERB + the OBJECT + RELATION + the DESTINATION ("put the coke can in the white bowl"). Target 10 words or fewer; never exceed 14. At most two clauses, and only in the form "pick up the X and put/place it <relation> the Y". Casing and final punctuation are free; nothing else is.

4. Use a plain relocation verb: put, place, move, pick up, take, set, stack, fold, unfold, open, close, lift, remove. Replace ornate or rare verbs (retrieve, position, arrange, relocate, deposit, transport, convey, elevate, grasp, hoist, locate, identify, ensure, execute) with the plain verb for the same action. "set" and "put" are interchangeable — do not avoid "set".

5. Name objects, never describe them. Replace any property or function description ("the carbonated drink container", "the long orange vegetable", "the device used for typing") with one plain name, chosen in this order:
   (a) the concrete noun the input itself uses elsewhere, or the scene trace identifies (coke can, carrot, keyboard);
   (b) if the object's true name is obscure and a common household noun matches what the object LOOKS like, use that common noun — a ramekin that looks like a small white bowl becomes "the white bowl";
   (c) if no common noun visually fits, keep the object's actual name even if it is unusual (keyboard, wheel) — an odd true name beats a wrong familiar name and beats any description.

6. Never introduce a vague category word as the reference: object, thing, item, element, stuff, container, vessel, receptacle. Replace a vague word from the input only when the input or trace lets you name the object concretely; if you cannot, keep the input's own wording. Emitting "the white object" for a nameable thing was the single worst measured move (about 4% success where the named version scored 64%).

7. Keep the input's exact nouns; never swap in a near-synonym and never name a part instead of the whole. Basket stays basket (not bin), plate stays plate (not dish), bowl stays bowl (not cup or dish), cube stays cube (not block), towel stays towel (not cloth), keyboard stays keyboard (not keys), wheel stays wheel (not rim). Single-token noun swaps are the largest effects in the whole record (14–61 points); when two reasonable words both fit, the input's word wins.

8. Keep specific and brand tokens exactly: "coke can" stays "coke can" — never generalize to cola, soda, beverage, or bare "can". Principle: the most specific short name the input gives is the name to keep; generalizing it always cost points, while even a wrong brand was harmless.

9. Adjectives: at most one basic color word per noun, and only colors the input already has or that are needed to tell two same-category objects apart. Simplify any compound or exotic color to its basic word (teal-green → green, yellowish-orange → yellow; basic set: red, blue, green, yellow, orange, purple, white, black, brown, pink, gray). Delete material words (ceramic, rubber, metal, metallic, plastic, wooden, stainless), texture words, and size words — keep a size word only when size alone distinguishes two otherwise-identical objects. Never add a color to an instruction that had none.

10. Spatial relation: keep the input's relation, in plain form — "in/into/inside" for entering a container, "on/onto" for resting on a surface, "off/out of" for removal. If the destination is hollow (bowl, pot, pan, basket, sink, drawer, box) and the input says "on", write "in". Do not add "on top of" where plain "on" stands; do not add "the center of" or "the middle of"; delete orientation words (upright, vertically, standing up) wherever they appear.

11. Delete — never reword — all wrapping and manner language: politeness and addressee framing (please, kindly, could you, I would appreciate, you are directed to, for me), conditionals and preambles (if, should, assuming, provided, once you are ready), justifications (in order to, so that), manner adverbs (carefully, gently, neatly, slowly, precisely, exactly, completely), and any commentary about the scene, the robot, or the task's importance. Exception: a bare "please" is measured harmless — deleting it is still always safe.

12. Delete scene-position qualifiers that merely locate things for a viewer ("on the left", "to its right", "nearby", "from the burner") unless position is the only way to tell two same-named objects apart. Accurate extra position detail still cost 25–40 points.

13. State the action, not the goal state and not a negation. "Move the bowl so it is no longer on the plate" becomes "take the bowl off the plate"; "ensure the can ends up inside the ramekin" becomes "put the coke can in the ramekin".

14. Name both ends. The moved object and its destination each get their own noun phrase — "stack the cubes" with no named target scored 0%. Preserve source-and-goal structure the task needs ("fold the cloth from bottom left to top right", "take the X out of the Y and put it on the Z") but add no waypoints beyond what the input's task requires.

15. Turn questions and second-person requests into bare imperatives: "Can you put the can of Coke in the ramekin for me?" → "put the coke can in the ramekin". Past-tense narration ("moved the silver pot to the left side of the table") is native corpus register — leave it, do not convert it.

16. Use the scene trace only to answer "what is this object called, and what basic color is it". Never copy trace phrasing into the output; simplify trace color words per rule 9. If the trace does not settle an object's identity, fall back to the input's own noun (rule 6).

17. Edit economy and collisions: make the fewest edits that clear the violations, prefer deletion over substitution, and never touch a compliant part of the sentence. Priority when rules collide: object naming first (rules 5–8 — noun edits dominate, 14–61 points), then deletion of wrappers, precision and position words (rules 10–13, typically 15–40 points), then verb and shape (rules 3–4), then length trimming last. If the only way to satisfy a rule is a risky substitution, leave that part unchanged instead.

===RATIONALE===
Triage (rule 2): the original plain instruction ranked best (score 1.0) in 182 of 219 proxy-measured tasks and near-best in almost all the rest; on rollouts, held-out layouts crowned the nominal 4 of 4 times on good-nominal tasks, and every search-split "improvement" regressed (winner's curse in every scene). So the book's strongest move is refusing to edit plain inputs.

Naming (rules 5–8): the one large confirmed rescue was naming — "put coke can on ramekin" 24% → "place the red coke can inside the white bowl" 88% (+64pp, looks-like rename plus in-relation). Descriptions cost −17 to −63pp everywhere they were tried; "the white object" collapsed to 4%. But keyboard and wheel showed the flip side: when nothing familiar fits, the true name is the ceiling — so the rename is licensed only by visual match, not by unfamiliarity alone. Synonym cliffs are the cleanest single-edit facts on record: basket→bin −38.9, plate→dish −30.5, bowl→cup −16.7, coke→cola −16.7, cube→block ±15, part-names (keys, rim) −24 to −29. Wrong brand (pepsi) was free while genericizing (soda) cost — hence "keep the specific token".

Adjectives (rule 9): teal-green vs green −33.4 (pure single edit), ceramic −37, rubber −13.9, and adding yellow/blue to good nominals −13.8/−19.4; color-only, simplify, don't add. Color did help inside multi-edit rescues on hard OOV scenes (black keyboard, black tire), which is why input colors are kept rather than stripped.

Wrappers, precision, position (rules 10–13): "exactly in the middle" −36, "in the middle of the towel" −41, "carefully/neatly" phrasings at ~0–5% in the 139-pair file, adversarial register at the bottom of nearly every task's proxy ranking, and the goal-state paraphrase dead last in its task. Accurate left/right detail cost −25 to −40 (case 6). "on top of" −16.6 on a good nominal; adding "upright" −8 to −10 pooled.

Verb and shape (rules 3–4, 14–15): "Arrange the eggplant neatly in the yellow bin" −40 vs plain; ornate verbs (retrieve/position/locate) at 0–3%; telegrams that drop the verb or a noun phrase −27 to −34; two-clause "pick up X and place it…" measured equal-or-better to one clause. v1's bans on "set" and "please" are repaired: set ≈ put across 5+ boards (set won the keyboard task), "please" measured free.

Priority (rule 17): noun-token effects (14–61pp) exceed every structural effect (8–40pp), and deletion is the only edit class never measured harmful — hence nouns first, delete before substitute, and the conservative fallback throughout.
```

**Analysis highlights beyond the named files:** I mined evidence.csv directly — the original instruction scores a perfect 1.0 (best in task) in 182 of 219 proxy tasks, and the per-task rankings consistently show ornate adversarials at the bottom, plain naturals near the top, and scene-detail additions ("from the burner", "small metal") ranking below even some adversarials. That's what makes rule 2's refuse-to-edit triage the book's backbone, with the v1 failure list dictating the repairs (set/please un-banned, upright deleted, brand tokens kept, vague-word fallback added).
